      "id": 1,
      "text": "Indio was founded in 1876 as Indian Wells on a former Palte‑Wat Indian village, renamed Indio in 1877.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Indio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first permanent structure was the Southern Pacific Depot Station and Hotel, built shortly after the town’s founding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.indio.org/our-city",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Coachella Valley History Museum houses the world’s only Date Museum, preserving the region’s fruit‑cultivation heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indio%2C_California",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s origin as a railroad stop arose from the need for a midway water refill between Yuma and Los Angeles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indio%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fantasy Springs Resort Casino, owned by the Cabazon Band of Mission Indians, sits in Indio and hosts the annual Cabazon Indian National Pow Wow.",
